22.2  Shell startup files

TL;DR
  - A login shell (console, SSH) reads the profile files. bash reads only the
    first that exists of ~/.bash_profile, ~/.bash_login and ~/.profile.
  - An interactive shell that isn't a login shell, such as a new terminal tab on
    most desktops, reads ~/.bashrc.
  - zsh reads ~/.zprofile for login shells and ~/.zshrc for every interactive
    shell.

      echo $0               # "-bash" with a dash: a login shell
      source ~/.bashrc      # reload after editing

On your machine
  Shell     /bin/zsh
  Files     ~/.bash_profile, ~/.profile, ~/.bashrc, ~/.zshrc, /etc/profile,
            /etc/profile.d/ (2 files)
  Bash      bash login shells read ~/.bash_profile and ignore ~/.profile

Pitfalls
  - When ~/.bash_profile exists, bash login shells ignore ~/.profile completely.
    Have ~/.bash_profile source ~/.bashrc to get both.
  - Scripts are not interactive: they read none of these files. Set what a
    script needs inside the script.
  - bash also reads ~/.bashrc for commands run over SSH: output from it (an
    echo) breaks scp and rsync. Print only in interactive shells.
  - A change to ~/.profile only applies at the next login, not in a new terminal
    tab.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 22.2 --why

Related
  Commands  source
  Paths     ~/.bashrc, ~/.profile, /etc/profile, /etc/profile.d/
  See also  idk 22.1 Where environment variables come from
  See also  idk 19.3 PATH and command resolution
